            {
                id terminal-translate
                title "Translate the terminal layer to Rust over terminfo"
                deps (terminal-markup)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T11:39:15.708180493Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Unfinished consequence of the capname substitution. Correcting my own earlier framing: I first called this a port defect outright, and half of it is not.\n\nThe tables moved from the C's termcap two-letter codes to terminfo capnames -- TSTR holds il1/bel/ed/el/hpa/clear/dch1 where tstr[] holds al/bl/cd/ce/ch/cl/dc. That is required by dec:libedit:terminal-caps-via-term-crate, since the term crate's maps are keyed by capname, and both tables are 39 entries in the same T_* index order.\n\nOUTPUT SIDE: already decided, not a defect. sem:terminal.terminal-telltc-fn explicitly raised it -- \"Changing the parenthesised token changes user-visible output, so it is a deliberate compatibility decision rather than a free choice\" -- the translation decided to substitute, and terminal.rs's TSTR doc comment records it. Leave it alone.\n\nINPUT SIDE: never addressed, and this is the actual gap. sem:terminal.terminal-settc-fn says only \"search the 39-entry string-capability table for an exact name match\" and never states which namespace the caller supplies. So changing the name column silently changed what a caller's string matches against. The rule prompted a decision about printing and nobody asked the same question about accepting.\n\nConsequence: `settc cl <value>` in a user's ~/.editrc, or el_set(el, EL_SETTC, \"cl\", value, NULL) from a program, resolves in the C and misses against us. Same for echotc and gettc, and for every capability rather than a few. It fails by silent lookup miss.\n\nThe functions are NOT exported -- terminal_settc, terminal_gettc, terminal_telltc and terminal_echotc are all libedit_private -- but the capability NAME crosses the public boundary through two doors: the histedit.h opcodes EL_TELLTC 5, EL_SETTC 6, EL_ECHOTC 7 and EL_GETTC 17, all documented as taking `const Char *`; and the editrc builtins at src/parse.c:70,73,74, reached from any ~/.editrc through el_source/el_parse. src/el.c:304-314 and src/eln.c:201-209 synthesize argv[0] and land on the same handlers.\n\nCalibration, since I overstated it first time: real, silent, affects every capability, but COLD. Hand-setting terminal capabilities from editrc or EL_SETTC is rare, and doc/editline.3 does not document settc or echotc at all.\n\nFIX: accept termcap names on input, keep capnames for the term crate lookup. The mapping needs no derivation -- it already exists three times over: the C's tstr[]/tval[] carry the termcap column at identical indices, our tables carry the capname at the same indices, and the sem rules spell out all three names per capability (\"terminfo clr_eol, capname el; termcap ce\"). A second field on Termcapstr/Termcapval, not a subsystem.\n\nWatch the buffer while doing it: settc copies the name into an 8-byte buffer with a truncating bounded copy, sized in the C for two-letter codes. Capnames fit -- clear, smdc, kcuu1 are all under 8 -- but the margin dropped from 6 spare bytes to 3, and sem:terminal.terminal-settc-fn describes that buffer as \"sized for two-letter capability names\", which is now only true of what it accepts and not of what it stores.\n\nBelongs here rather than in a new leaf: the terminal concern is sealed at four waves and this is translation that is not finished. nplan_add refused a fifth child, correctly."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T13:34:08.399932152Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "tgoto's workaround is now redundant, reported by the agent that removed the bug it worked around (commit c146bb7).\n\ncrates/nshedit/src/terminal.rs's tgoto, around lines 688-756, splits the capability at every `$`, expands the runs between with expand_into, and copies padding runs through by hand. It did that because nshterm's ancestor discarded `$<...>` and swallowed text after a bare `$`. nshterm::parm::expand now passes padding through verbatim in one pass, so the split is unnecessary.\n\nIt is still CORRECT -- nothing is broken and there is no urgency. But its own doc comment records a latent cost the fix removes: \"`%i` and the `%P`/`%g` variables apply within a run, and `vars` is threaded across runs but the parameters are re-supplied unincremented to each\". That is a real semantic difference from a single expand call, currently invisible because no shipped capability puts `%i` after a padding run. Collapsing tgoto to one expand call deletes both the caveat and about 50 lines.\n\nDo this together with the termcap-name work already noted on this leaf rather than as its own pass -- both are in the same function's neighbourhood and both are cheap once someone is in there.\n\nVerify with the conformance differential, which is at zero divergences: any change here must keep it there. Note the current driver does not reach a parameterised capability, so the differential will NOT catch a regression in tgoto. conformance-driver-el is what would, since EL_TELLTC and the editrc path exercise capability handling. Either write the driver first or test tgoto directly; do not rely on a green differential as evidence for this one."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T17:28:14.034714683Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id terminal-tests
                title "Write tests for the terminal layer from its sem rules; there are none to port"
                deps (terminal-translate platform-build)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:29:46.200708863Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two findings from writing these, both about documents rather than code.\n\nTHE RULE PROSE FOR sem:tty.tty-printchar-fn IS WRONG ABOUT THE C. It says the `i % 5 == 0` newline \"fires on i == 0, so the output starts with a blank line\". In src/tty.c the newline check comes after the fprintf of the entry, not before it, so the output starts \"intr ^A \\n\" and the grouping is offset by one. There is no leading blank line. The port matches the C; only the rule is wrong. Not corrected here: six other test nodes were mid-flight against the current spec when this was found, and bumping a rule version cascades every node implementing it to stale. It belongs in a spec-correction pass with the others, and the test written for it pins the C's real output with the offset explained.\n\nTTY_STTY'S DISPLAY WRAP DOUBLE-COUNTS. The wrap test is `len + cu >= width`, and `len` already includes the group label, so a width-20 terminal wraps where hand-arithmetic says it fits. Worse, a wrapped line resumes at `st + cu` rather than at `st` — the entry just emitted is counted a second time — so a fresh line with room for two entries immediately wraps again. That is why narrow `stty` output is one entry per line. Carried faithfully from the C. This was the one expectation written from reading rather than running that turned out wrong, and it is exactly the class of error a hand-computed expectation produces.\n\nThe tests need no terminfo database and never call terminal_init or terminal_set: every capability is installed through terminal_alloc directly, and every descriptor is forced below zero. A test that read TERM would answer differently on each machine, which is the failure mode this layer invites."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:29:54.224249112Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id terminal-idiomatize
                title "Idiomatize the terminal layer"
                deps (terminal-tests)
                sealed true
            }
        )
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-05T11:34:22.552572706Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "PORT DEFECT, found 2026-08-05 by asking what libtinfo was for. Not a C defect, so not an errata entry. Needs fixing before the terminal concern can be called translated.\n\nDebian's libedit.so.2 imports six symbols from libtinfo: tgetent, tgetflag, tgetnum, tgetstr, tgoto, tputs. That is the termcap client API, and ncurses' libtinfo provides it as an emulation layer over its terminfo database. Dropping the library therefore means taking on the emulation ourselves, and one half of that has been done in a way that breaks the other.\n\nTHE CAPABILITY NAMES DIVERGED, AND THEY ARE USER-VISIBLE ON INPUT.\n\nsrc/terminal.c's tstr[] holds termcap two-letter names -- \"al\", \"bl\", \"cd\", \"ce\", \"ch\", \"cl\", \"dc\" -- because that is what tgetstr takes. crates/nshedit/src/terminal.rs's TSTR holds terminfo capnames -- \"il1\", \"bel\", \"ed\", \"el\", \"hpa\", \"clear\", \"dch1\" -- because that is what the term crate's maps are keyed by. Reading the database that way is correct. The problem is that the same table is what user input is matched against:\n\n    port:  TSTR.iter().position(|t| t.name.as_bytes() == what)\n    C:     for (ts = tstr; ts->name; ts++) if (strcmp(ts->name, what) == 0)\n\nBoth are a linear match of the caller's string against the table's name field. So `settc cl <value>` in an existing .editrc, or el_set(EL_SETTC, \"cl\", ...) from a program, resolves in the C and finds NOTHING against us. Same for echotc and for every capability, not a handful. It fails silently -- the lookup just misses -- which is the worst shape for a drop-in break.\n\nThere is no alias layer today. The doc comment at terminal.rs:210 acknowledges the difference (\"(`\\\"al\\\"`, `\\\"bl\\\"`, ...), terminfo long name here\") and is itself doubly wrong: it is a capname, not a long name, and noting the divergence is not the same as handling it.\n\nFix: a termcap-to-terminfo alias at the lookup boundary. Keep the tables keyed by capname, since that is what the database needs, and accept the C's two-letter names from users and from editrc. That is exactly what ncurses' emulation does, and the mapping is well defined -- terminfo's own tables carry the termcap name for each capability.\n\nRelated and already noted on errata-reconcile-2, but that entry only covers the OUTPUT side: EL_TELLTC prints al/bl/cd/ce/ch where we print il1/bel/ed/el/hpa. Cosmetic next to this. conformance-driver-el will surface both.\n\nSecond inherited piece, separate and smaller: tputs padding. The term crate's parm::expand discards $<...> delays, and t_speed is 0 anyway while tcgetattr is stubbed, so no padding is emitted at all. Conformant under the rule's zero-speed clause, but unreachable rather than correct -- see platform-layer divergence group 3.\n\nAlso worth knowing for packaging: dropping libtinfo drops the LIBRARY, not the terminfo DATABASE. /usr/share/terminfo is still required at runtime and ncurses is still what ships it."
            }
        )
    }

