                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T17:28:06.867516484Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Completing this with one function deliberately not translated, and saying so rather than letting the node look cleaner than it is.\n\n`crates/nshedit/src/search.rs:347` is the only `todo!()` left anywhere in the workspace — measured, not assumed: `grep -rn 'todo!\\|unimplemented!'` over every crate returns exactly that one site. It is `ce_search_line`'s regex path, and it is not translatable in the way the rest of this node was: the C calls `regcomp`/`regexec`, and `dec:libedit:no-c-ffi` gives the core crate no route to either. It needs an engine chosen, which is a decision, not a translation.\n\nThat decision already has a node — `history-regex`, which deps on this one. So the carve-out is in the graph rather than in someone's memory, and completing this is what makes that node startable.\n\nEverything else in the concern is done and measured: the wave-2 gate reads 650/650 per symbol, and the conformance differential drives history and the tokenizer end to end at 247 operations under C.UTF-8 and 246 under C, identical to an oracle built from this tree's own C — which covers H_ENTER, H_ADD, H_APPEND, H_DEL, H_SAVE, H_LOAD, H_NSAVE_FP, the narrow and wide instantiations, and every byte of the saved history file.\n\nThe same reasoning applies to the six sibling translate nodes being completed alongside this one. None of them has a stub; the gate that measures them is per-symbol and full; and the frontier was handing back work the manifest already called done, which made `nplan_next` unusable as a source of what to do next. That is the actual cost of leaving these open, and it is why they are being closed now rather than at the end of the wave."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T17:28:14.105422139Z
