            {
                id history-regex
                title "Choose and integrate a basic-regex engine for history search"
                deps (history-translate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T08:19:37.774689188Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two facts from src/search.c:118-163 that narrow the engine choice, both missing from the comment on `bre_match`:\n\n1. The regex leg is a FALLBACK, not the primary path. `el_match` opens with `if (wcsstr(str, pat) != 0) return 1;` — a plain wide substring test — and only reaches `regcomp`/`regexec` when the literal substring is absent. Interactive history search is overwhelmingly literal, so engine choice governs a cold path, not a hot one. A heavier or slower engine is affordable here.\n\n2. Matching happens on ENCODED BYTES, not code points. Both operands go through `ct_encode_string(..., &conv)` before `regcomp`/`regexec`, so the C matches in the locale's multibyte encoding. A byte-oriented engine is therefore the faithful choice; a `char`-oriented one would diverge on any multibyte pattern. This also means bracket expressions and `.` operate on bytes, so `.` matches one byte of a UTF-8 sequence, not one character.\n\nFailure semantics: `regcomp` returning non-zero yields `rv = 0` — an unparseable pattern is simply \"no match\", never an error to the caller. cflags is 0, so POSIX BRE with no REG_NEWLINE and no REG_ICASE.\n\nAlso confirmed and NOT a defect: `pat` and `str` are encoded through the same static `conv` buffer, so the second `ct_encode_string` overwrites the first. This is safe only because POSIX `regcomp` copies the pattern rather than retaining the pointer. Worth an errata note as a latent trap for any port that swaps in an engine holding a borrow on the pattern."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T21:03:50.160526017Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id history-record-blob
                title "Give a history entry an owned (text, blob) record, so the app data can be persisted at all"
                priority 1
                kind @feat
                deps (history-format-native)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T15:13:40.085393283Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T15:14:33.507851758Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "REVERSED the dep between this node and history-format-native, ten minutes after authoring it the wrong way round. This now depends on the container rather than the container depending on it.\n\nTwo things I had not looked at when I wrote the original edge:\n\n1. `crates/nshedit/src/history.rs` is 2583 lines of literal wave-2 port, holding entries on raw pointers with `Strdup`/`h_free` and a circular intrusive list. Changing `HentryGen`'s representation is an invasive refactor of a file whose own translate node is still open, and it would fight the port rather than build on it.\n\n2. More decisive: the blob has no meaning on the C ABI path at all. `HentryGen.data` is a `*mut c_void` the caller owns and libedit hands back untouched — there is no length, so there is nothing to serialize even in principle. A caller reaching us through `histedit.h` cannot supply a persistable blob, and inventing a length for it would be making up an ABI libedit does not have.\n\nSo the blob is a Rust-native concern, for nsh, and the entry change is the WIRING step, not the foundation. The container — records, framing, the reader — is standalone, has no dependency on how entries are held in memory, and is testable on its own. Building it first also means the format is settled before anything invasive touches the port.\n\nThe practical effect on order: history-format-native is now the ready node and this one follows it. Nothing else in the group moves."
                    }
                )
            }

