                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T18:30:29.574929547Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "REOPENING. I completed this node earlier today on insufficient evidence, and the first run of the new el_api conformance driver disproved it.\n\nWhat I checked: `grep -rn 'todo!\\|unimplemented!'` over every crate, which returned one site in search.rs, plus the wave-2 gate reading 650/650. Both were true and neither was sufficient.\n\nWhat the driver measured: 22 of 88 operations diverge, and the shape says one cause rather than twenty. Every el_set and el_get returns -1 on the port where the oracle returns 0 — except `EL_EDITOR bogus`, which both reject, so the entry point is reached and the argument is seen.\n\n    0003 EL_EDITOR emacs      oracle rc=0    port rc=-1\n    0007 EL_EDITOR bogus      oracle rc=-1   port rc=-1\n    0009 EL_SIGNAL 1          oracle rc=0    port rc=-1\n    0022 EL_GET terminal      oracle rc=0 <dumb>   port rc=-1 <(null)>\n\nThe cause is written down in the code, at crates/nshedit-abi/src/eln.rs:434: \"Step 2 and the whole dispatch are unwritten, so every op takes the C's default arm.\" The comment then specifies, in detail and correctly, what each arm owes — it is a deliberate, well-documented stub awaiting the varargs work, not an oversight.\n\nWhy no gate caught it. It has a body, it returns a value, and the value is right for an unsupported op, so stub-scan does not fire. The wave-2 gate measures whether an ANNOTATION is present, and `[spec:libedit:def:eln.el-set-fn]` sits on the function — so 650/650 counts a stub as translated. That is the gap this whole wave-3 exercise exists to close, and it is worth stating that the gate cannot close it on its own: only executing the code does.\n\nTwo things follow.\n\nThe narrow el_set/el_get dispatch is the remaining work on this node, and abi-varargs is what unblocks it — the arms read their arguments with `ap.next_arg::<T>()`, which is why they were left until c_variadic landed. It has landed.\n\nMore generally: completing a translate node needs evidence that the code RUNS, not that it exists. The six siblings I closed alongside this one were closed on the same reasoning and deserve the same suspicion. conformance-driver-readline and conformance-driver-ub are the instruments for that, and this is now a reason to build them rather than a nice-to-have."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id core-api-tests
                title "Write tests for the EditLine lifecycle and public API from their sem rules; there are none to port"
                deps (
                    core-api-translate
                    abi-callback-types
                    abi-visibility
                    abi-varargs
                    abi-narrow
                    platform-build
                )
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:35:40.475369128Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:36:03.151628913Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Four things this pass found that are not test code, kept together because three of them are documents that have drifted behind the code and one is a real gap.\n\nA REAL FUNCTIONAL GAP IN THE NARROW el_get. There is no arm for EL_EDITOR, EL_WORDCHARS, EL_PROMPT, EL_RPROMPT, EL_PROMPT_ESC or EL_RPROMPT_ESC, so el_get answers -1 and writes nothing where the C answers 0 and writes a value. This is a divergence we introduced, not a defect of the C we reproduced, and the setter side handles the same ops — abi-elset-dispatch built the decode arm for el_set and stopped. Needs its own node under abi-surface; the test written for it documents the current answer with a comment saying so rather than asserting the C's.\n\nTHE eln.rs MODULE HEADER IS FALSE. It says el_set and el_get \"dispatch on no op ... every op falls to the C's default arm and comes back -1\". That was true before abi-elset-dispatch and has not been true since: el_set_va has the forwarded set, the five list ops, EL_HIST, the prompt family and the EL_EDITOR/EL_WORDCHARS decode, and el_get_va forwards nine. A reader trusting the header would conclude the narrow API is a stub.\n\ndocs/errata.md CARRIES THE SAME DRIFT. ERR-core-api-09 still reads \"open — not carried out ... el_set returns -1 for every op\", but the NULL rejections it prescribes are implemented and are now asserted. ERR-core-api-29 still reads \"the narrow el_get's half is still a comment only — that dispatch is a blanket -1 stub\", but EL_GETTC is in FORWARDED_TO_WGET and reads `li` back. Both statuses are wrong about the tree.\n\nsem:histedit.el-beep-fn MISDESCRIBES THE C. It says the terminal layer emits a visible-bell capability, otherwise the audible bell string, and writes nothing if neither is available. src/terminal.c:864 tests only T_bl and writes a literal 0x07 otherwise: there is no visible-bell branch and it never writes nothing. The port follows the C; the rule is wrong. Left uncorrected for now because five other test nodes were mid-flight against the current spec and a rule-version bump cascades their nodes to stale. It belongs with sem:tty.tty-printchar-fn, which terminal-tests found wrong the same way, in one spec-correction pass.\n\nTwo measurements that had to be made rather than reasoned about. EL_SETFP cannot be handed a fabricated FILE *: el_init_fd stores the three streams verbatim without touching them, but el_wset routes through cstdio::fileno_of, which calls the real fileno(3) and dereferences. The only testable argument is NULL; distinct sentinel streams have to go in through the constructor and come back through EL_GETFP. And EL_SAFEREAD answers 256 — the raw FIXIO bit, not a boolean; EL_SIGNAL only looks like it round-trips because HANDLE_SIGNALS is 0x001."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id core-api-idiomatize
                title "Idiomatize the EditLine lifecycle and public API"
                deps (core-api-tests)
                sealed true
            }
        )
        sealed true
    }

