            {
                id platform-plat-tests
                title "Test nshedit-plat itself, the one crate where a wrong constant is silent and total"
                deps (platform-build)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T17:45:17.786274439Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Debt we created, not debt we inherited, which is why this gets its own node rather than riding on platform-tests. crates/nshedit-plat/src measures 0.094 test-to-production against a 0.200 floor, bypassed at the platform-build commit.\n\nWhat is already there and is genuinely good: five tests including a sigaction install/raise/restore round-trip -- the only thing that can catch a mis-transcribed struct -- and compile-time layout assertions checked against gcc's own offsets (sigaction 152 bytes, sigset_t 128, passwd 48). That is the highest-value testing for this crate and it is why the number understates the coverage.\n\nWhy the number still matters here more than elsewhere. This crate is two libc extern blocks and a rustix wrapper layer, and its failure mode is not a wrong answer but a silently wrong one: a struct field at the wrong offset, a V* subscript off by one, a flag word with the wrong bit. platform-build already found exactly that class of bug in the CORE -- blank_termios building a zero-length c_cc, which cc_get and cc_set swallowed silently because they are bounds-safe -- and that one was invisible for as long as the syscall beneath it was stubbed. The same shape of mistake inside nshedit-plat has nothing beneath it to be invisible against.\n\nWhat to aim at, roughly in order of how badly a mistake would hurt:\n- The 17-entry V* subscript table in termios.rs. rustix's SpecialCodeIndex cannot be constructed from a number, so this table was written by hand from the kernel's names; an off-by-one here silently rebinds the user's erase or interrupt character. Assert every entry against the C's own header rather than against the table's author.\n- plat::tcsetattr's read-then-overwrite. It exists because rustix uses TCSETS2 and libedit's termios has no c_ispeed/c_ospeed; the test that matters is that a line at an arbitrary BOTHER speed survives a call that never meant to touch the speed.\n- The signal trampoline's AtomicPtr slot, specifically the ordering: sig_set publishes inside the blocked window and sig_clr clears, which the C never does. Concurrency here is not theoretical -- the handler runs on whatever thread the kernel picks.\n- getpwnam_r's ERANGE conflation, which sem:filecomplete.fn-tilde-expand-fn requires be preserved.\n\nDo not lower the floor. And note the conformance harness cannot help: it is headless and drives the C ABI, where this crate sits underneath. The PTY differential platform-build built separately is the closest thing and it is not in the tree -- worth recovering into conformance-pty rather than rewriting."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

