            {
                id abi-varargs
                title "Make el_set, el_get and history genuinely variadic, now that c_variadic is stable in 1.99"
                priority 0
                deps (abi-visibility abi-callback-types)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T15:37:59.4470987Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "USE REAL C VARIADICS. This reverses the fixed-arity constraint I established earlier in the port, which is now stale.\n\nMeasured today rather than recalled. On stable 1.97.1 the feature is still gated -- E0658, \"C-variadic functions are unstable\". On the installed nightly, rustc 1.99.0-nightly (8ab9fdff5, 2026-07-30), the compiler says outright:\n\n    warning: the feature `c_variadic` has been stable since 1.99.0-nightly\n             and no longer requires an attribute to enable\n\nSo it is stabilised, not merely available, and lands in stable 1.99 -- two releases out from the 1.97.1 this workspace builds on.\n\nThe API changed with stabilisation and the old shape does not compile. `VaListImpl` is gone; `...` now desugars directly to `VaList<'_>`, and the accessor is `next_arg::<T>()`, not `arg::<T>()`. Anything written against the pre-stabilisation API needs updating.\n\nProven end to end, not assumed -- this compiles on nightly, exports the symbol, and forwards to a non-variadic dispatcher, which is exactly the shape el_set and el_get need:\n\n    pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn el_set_like(op: c_int, ap: ...) -> c_int {\n        unsafe { dispatch(op, ap) }\n    }\n    unsafe extern \"C\" fn dispatch(op: c_int, mut ap: VaList<'_>) -> c_int {\n        match op {\n            1 => unsafe { ap.next_arg::<c_int>() },\n            2 => unsafe { ap.next_arg::<*mut c_void>() }.addr() as c_int,\n            _ => -1,\n        }\n    }\n\nWhat this buys, and it is not cosmetic. The fixed-arity shims were correct on x86-64 SysV and aarch64 AAPCS and WRONG on aarch64 Apple, where variadic arguments go on the stack rather than in registers -- a caveat recorded in nshedit-abi's module docs and in no-c-ffi's deferred list. Real variadics delete that divergence rather than documenting it. el_set, el_get and their narrow twins can then match histedit.h exactly instead of approximating it, which also removes the reason a shipped header would have had to disagree with libedit's -- see abi-headers.\n\nIt also unblocks the narrow el_set/el_get, which errata-reconcile-2 verified are still a blanket -1 in eln.rs and which the register's remaining \"missing facility\" lines all point at.\n\nThe cost, stated plainly: a nightly toolchain until 1.99 ships. For a library other people link that is a real constraint, not a formality -- it wants a rust-toolchain.toml pinning a dated nightly so the build is reproducible, and Cargo.toml's rust-version rises to 1.99 when it lands. Two releases is a short wait; if the timing is wrong, the fixed-arity shims still work everywhere except aarch64 Apple and this can be deferred without blocking anything else."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T15:54:10.192470784Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "FIXED ARITY IS OBSOLETE. Use real C-variadic functions. This reverses the constraint I set earlier in the port, and I set it on a measurement that has since expired.\n\nWhat I measured then: rustc 1.97.1 rejects `extern \"C\" fn f(n: c_int, ap: ...)` with E0658, \"C-variadic functions are unstable\", pointing at issue #44930. True, and still true of stable today.\n\nWhat is true now, measured on the nightly already installed here: rustc 1.99.0-nightly (8ab9fdff5, 2026-07-30) accepts it and emits a warning saying so outright -- \"the feature `c_variadic` has been stable since 1.99.0-nightly and no longer requires an attribute to enable\". No feature gate needed. Stable 1.97.1, beta 1.98, nightly 1.99, so it reaches stable two releases out.\n\nProven end to end rather than assumed, with the exact shape el_set needs:\n\n    pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn el_set_like(op: c_int, ap: ...) -> c_int {\n        unsafe { dispatch(op, ap) }\n    }\n    unsafe extern \"C\" fn dispatch(op: c_int, mut ap: VaList<'_>) -> c_int {\n        match op {\n            1 => unsafe { ap.next_arg::<c_int>() },\n            2 => unsafe { ap.next_arg::<*mut c_void>() }.addr() as c_int,\n            _ => -1,\n        }\n    }\n\nCompiles as a cdylib and exports `T el_set_like`. Two API notes for whoever writes this, both of which cost me a compile each: the method is `next_arg`, not `arg`; and `VaListImpl` is gone -- `...` desugars straight to `VaList<'_>`, so forwarding to a non-variadic dispatcher is just passing the value along, which is exactly the structure el_set wants.\n\nWHAT THIS BUYS, and it is not cosmetic. The fixed-arity shim is correct on x86-64 SysV and aarch64 AAPCS and WRONG on aarch64 Apple, where variadic arguments go on the stack rather than in registers -- a caller and callee disagreeing about where an argument lives, which is silent corruption rather than a link error. Real variadics delete that caveat instead of documenting it. They also make the shipped header able to declare el_set and el_get exactly as histedit.h does, which is abi-headers' problem too.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS: nightly until 1.99 ships. For a library other people link that is a real constraint, not a shrug, and it wants a rust-toolchain.toml pinning the nightly rather than tracking it. Cargo.toml currently says rust-version = \"1.85\", which becomes a lie the moment this lands and must move with it.\n\nSequencing: this is worth doing BEFORE abi-headers rather than after. The header has to declare these functions one way or the other, and declaring them variadic while the implementation is fixed-arity is the mismatch we would be shipping."
                    }

