            {
                id terminal-change-size
                title "Fix terminal_change_size, which fails where the C succeeds, so every settc co/li reports an error"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T19:37:18.431211169Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "PARKED UNDER THE WRONG PARENT. This is terminal-layer work and belongs under `terminal`; it sits under `abi-surface` because `terminal`'s decomposition carries `sealed true` and both `nplan_add --parent terminal` and `nplan_mv` refuse it. That refusal is the seal working — it means \"these children are the whole of this work\" — so the fix is an explicit unseal by someone who decides that claim is no longer true, not a quiet insert somewhere the graph will not notice. Move it when that happens.\n\nTHE DEFECT, traced from the harness rather than guessed at.\n\nconformance/driver/el_api.c reports two remaining divergences out of 88:\n\n    0038 parse: <settc co 132>   oracle parse=0   port parse=1\n    0039 parse: <settc li 50>    oracle parse=0   port parse=1\n\n`el_wparse` returns `-i` where `i` is the builtin's return — src/parse.c, `i = (*cmds[i].func)(el, argc, argv); return -i;` — so `parse=1` means the builtin returned -1 and `parse=0` means it returned 0. The port's `terminal_settc` is therefore failing where the C succeeds.\n\nIts only failure path past argument validation is the last one:\n\n    if size_changed && terminal_change_size(el, val(el, T_LI), val(el, T_CO)) == -1 {\n        return -1;\n    }\n\nand `size_changed` is true for exactly `co` and `li`, which is exactly the two operations that diverge. `settc` on any other capability agrees. So `terminal_change_size` returns -1 in the port and 0 in the C, on a capability that exists and a value that parses.\n\nNot investigated further, and I stopped rather than dig at the end of a session: what it does next is grow the display arrays, which is where a real difference would live and where a wrong guess would be expensive.\n\nWorth noting what this says about the harness. The el_api driver was written to find missing coverage and it has now produced two distinct real defects — el_set's whole dispatch, and this — neither of which any gate could see, because both are functions that return a plausible value. That is the argument for building conformance-driver-readline and conformance-driver-ub rather than annotating wave 3 from reading."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T19:40:22.777789572Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "CORRECTION: terminal_change_size is NOT the fault; ignore that lead. terminal_alloc_buffer returns Some unconditionally and has no failure path, so terminal_alloc_display, terminal_rebuffer_display and terminal_change_size all always return 0. I took the last -1 before the tail of terminal_settc without reading down to the leaf.\n\nStill established, both measured: el_wparse returns the negation of the builtin's return, so parse=1 does mean terminal_settc returned -1; and the divergence is confined to co and li, since settc on every other capability in the corpus agrees.\n\nNext place to look: terminal_settc's other -1, the TVAL capability lookup. co and li are the two TVAL entries the driver exercises, so a failing name comparison for exactly those two fits every observation. Compare the encoded argument against the table entry byte for byte before assuming the table is right. Note the Bad capability message goes to el_errfile, which the driver points at /dev/null, so its absence from the trace proves nothing.\n\nSame class as the two capname typos found in nshterm today: a table entry no test compares against a reference. The id is now known to be wrong; rename once the cause is confirmed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T19:47:11.977647343Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "CAUSE FOUND, and it is a deliberate decision that is right for output and wrong for input.\n\ncrates/nshedit/src/terminal.rs:297, TVAL, against src/terminal.c's tval:\n\n    C     am  pt  li     co     km  xt  xn\n    port  am  pt  lines  cols   km  xt  xenl\n\nThe port substituted terminfo capnames for the C's termcap codes. terminal.rs:244 says so and calls it deliberate, citing sem:terminal.terminal-telltc-fn as recording it as a user-visible substitution.\n\nThat explains every observation exactly. settc co 132 looks up co, TVAL holds cols, the lookup misses, terminal_settc reports Bad capability and returns -1, and el_wparse negates it to 1. The four entries that agree — am, pt, km, xt — agree because those two spellings coincide. li and co are the only two whose spellings differ AND that the driver exercises, which is why the divergence is exactly two operations.\n\nThe decision is defensible for OUTPUT. telltc prints capability names, and printing terminfo names in a port that reads terminfo is a reasonable thing to choose.\n\nIt is wrong for INPUT. settc and echotc take a name a user typed, at a prompt or in a .editrc, and what users type is termcap — that is what libedit's own documentation shows and what every .editrc already on disk contains. A user whose .editrc says settc co 132 gets Bad capability from us and silence from libedit.\n\nThe fix already exists as of today. nshterm-termcap-names added nshterm::parser::names::capname_for_termcap, generated from ncurses' include/Caps, precisely so a user-typed termcap code can be resolved. terminal_settc and terminal_echotc should try the argument as a termcap code and translate before reporting Bad capability. TVAL can keep terminfo names, so telltc's output is unchanged and the recorded decision stands.\n\nNote the scope is wider than the two operations the driver caught: every numeric and string capability whose termcap and terminfo spellings differ is affected on the settc and echotc paths, which is most of them. The driver found two because its corpus exercises two.\n\nOnce fixed, rename this node — the id says terminal-change-size, which was my first and wrong diagnosis."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T20:06:33.062661507Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-05T08:39:16.111071698Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The vis/unvis C surface must NOT be exported. This corrects a conclusion reached during abi-errno, which held that \"the real libedit.so on Linux compiles vis.c/unvis.c and exports the symbols\" and proposed a plan node to export them. Measured on this host, the opposite is true.\n\n`nm -D /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libedit.so.2` defines 205 symbols, none of them vis-related, and carries three UNDEFINED imports:\n\n    U strunvis@LIBBSD_0.0\n    U strvis@LIBBSD_0.0\n    U vis@LIBBSD_0.0\n\nSo Debian's libedit gets vis from libbsd. `src/Makefile.in:97-98` gates the sources on `@HAVE_VIS_FALSE@`/`@HAVE_UNVIS_FALSE@`, and with libbsd present the configure probe succeeds, so vis.c and unvis.c are never compiled in. glibc itself has no strvis — its only 'vis' symbols are madvise/fadvise relatives.\n\nTwo consequences:\n\n1. No plan node is needed for exporting the vis family, and none should be added. The drop-in contract is what libedit.so.2 actually exports, which excludes them.\n\n2. Exporting them anyway would be actively harmful, not merely redundant. Our artifact is installed with libedit.so.0 symlinked onto it. A process that loads both it and libbsd would then have two definitions of strvis in scope, and ELF interposition binds by load order, so libbsd's own callers could silently start calling ours. That is the same interposition hazard ERR-readline-53 already records for `completion_matches`, arriving from the other direction.\n\nWhat remains true from the abi-errno finding: the core's vis/unvis engine records ENOSPC/ENOMEM/EINVAL faithfully, and no C caller can observe those through us because we export no entry point that would. That is correct rather than a gap — the values are observable only through our own history-file path, which is an internal caller.\n\nOne thing this does raise, and it is NOT resolved: our vis/unvis is translated from NetBSD's vis.c, but the history files a Debian user already has on disk were written by libedit calling *libbsd's* vis. If those two disagree on any encoding, we misread existing history files. libbsd's vis derives from NetBSD's so they are probably identical, but \"probably\" is not a basis for a format we must read. Worth a differential test against libbsd before the history file format is declared done."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-05T14:00:59.621745347Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Four stale cross-file citations, found by errata-reconcile-2 and deliberately NOT repaired from the register — it is a C-defects register and a list of stale port-side citations sits outside that charter. Recorded here so they are attributable rather than quietly fixed. All four are prose; no behaviour is wrong.\n\n1. crates/nshedit-abi/src/histedit.rs, history_dispatch's H_NSAVE_FP arm, cites ERR-history-15 for the `n + 1` off-by-one. That is ERR-history-19. ERR-history-15 is history_def_del's ownership transfer — a different defect entirely, so the citation points a reader at the wrong thing rather than merely at nothing.\n\n2. Same file, el_wset's EL_TERMINAL arm around line 946: \"and also -1, which sem:histedit.el-wset-fn does not say\". The rule has said it since 55258de. This one was predicted when that commit landed and is now confirmed still present.\n\n3. conformance/driver/hist_tok.c, file header and section 0, still describe the eight narrow entry points as aborting through core_gap(), with line numbers into histedit.rs. The MEASUREMENT is right — both traces report `survived, exit=0` since abi-narrow landed in 8af6682 — so the harness is not lying, only its commentary is. Worth fixing before someone reads the header and believes the differential still has known divergences.\n\n4. crates/nshedit/src/terminal.rs, tgoto: its comment describes term's delay state as current behaviour, and the cut-at-every-$ segmentation it justifies is redundant since c146bb7. Already noted on terminal-translate with the wider point that collapsing tgoto to one expand call removes about 50 lines and a documented semantic caveat.\n\nItems 1 and 2 belong to whoever next opens nshedit-abi/src/histedit.rs — likely abi-varargs. Item 3 belongs with the next conformance driver, since that file gets edited then anyway. Item 4 is already on terminal-translate.\n\nWorth noting the general shape: all four are comments that were true when written and were falsified by a later commit, and none of them could be caught by a gate. The commit that invalidates a comment elsewhere in the tree is the one that should update it, and that is easier to say than to do when the invalidation is three files away."
            }
        )
    }

