            {
                id conformance-header-diff
                title "Diff a generated header against libedit's, which is the only check that would catch struct-layout drift"
                deps (conformance-oracle abi-headers)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T15:55:30.456024642Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The gap this closes, stated plainly because it is currently invisible: conformance/abi-shape.sh compares exported symbol NAMES. Nothing in the harness checks a signature, and nothing checks a struct LAYOUT.\n\nSo if our LineInfo, LineInfoW, HistEvent or HistEventW has a field in the wrong order or the wrong width, every C consumer that reads those structs gets garbage, the symbol table is byte-identical, and all five conformance stages stay green. That is the worst shape a defect can have here -- silent, total for the affected consumer, and invisible to every gate we own.\n\nMethod: generate a header from the ABI crate with cbindgen, diff against the installed /usr/include/histedit.h and editline/*.h, and fail on any difference not on an adjudicated allow-list. Each entry on that list needs a reason, the way abi-shape.sh's decided-symbols list does -- and for the same purpose, so the list cannot quietly absorb a new divergence.\n\nTwo things to expect rather than be surprised by. cbindgen describes OUR Rust, not libedit's contract, so cosmetic differences will be numerous on the first run: typedef spelling, ordering, and whether the EL_* opcodes emerge as #define or enum. That last one is not cosmetic -- consumers write `#ifdef EL_PROMPT`, so an enum breaks them. Expect to spend the first pass separating \"our types are wrong\" from \"cbindgen renders it differently\", and record which is which.\n\nDepends on abi-headers because that node decides what the shipped header IS. Diffing before that is settled would measure drift against a target we have not chosen. Depends on the oracle because the honest comparison is against the header for the version we ported, not whatever the host happens to have installed -- the oracle build already produces one.\n\nRelated and NOT covered by this: SONAME. abi-soname's note asks for that assertion in abi-shape.sh, which is a different file and a different stage."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T17:55:06.349201042Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "CORRECTION to the direction recorded on this node. See the same correction on abi-headers.\n\nThe generated header is what ships. This stage does not check a hand-written header against the code -- it checks THE CODE against the original .h, by generating a header from the ABI crate with cbindgen and diffing that against the oracle's histedit.h and editline/*.h. A difference means our Rust is wrong, or it is a divergence we have decided; it never means the generated header needs hand-editing, because nothing hand-edits it.\n\nThe gap this closes is unchanged and is still the reason the node exists: conformance/abi-shape.sh compares exported symbol NAMES. Nothing in the harness checks a signature and nothing checks a struct LAYOUT. A field in the wrong order inside LineInfo, LineInfoW, HistEvent or HistEventW breaks every C consumer that reads it, leaves the symbol table byte-identical, and passes all five stages. That is the worst shape a defect can have here, and platform-build proved the shape is real rather than theoretical -- blank_termios's zero-length c_cc was silent for exactly as long as the layer beneath it was stubbed.\n\nPractical consequence of the inversion, and the thing to get right first: spend the opening effort on cbindgen CONFIG rather than on an allow-list. Rendering differences are bugs in the config -- #define versus enum for the EL_* opcodes is the load-bearing one, since consumers write `#ifdef EL_PROMPT`, and an enum silently breaks them. Typedef spelling and declaration order are the same kind of thing. Only once the config is right does a remaining difference mean something, and those go on an allow-list WITH REASONS, the way abi-shape.sh's decided-symbols list works, so it cannot quietly absorb a new divergence. A long allow-list means the config is wrong.\n\nExpect to change Rust type definitions to make the output match. That is the intended direction of pressure, not a smell.\n\nDependencies stand: conformance-oracle, because the honest comparison is against the header for the version we ported rather than whatever the host has installed; and abi-headers, because that node decides what ships. abi-varargs is now done, so el_set and el_get can be declared variadic in the generated header exactly as the implementation is -- which was the sequencing reason abi-headers was gated on it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T18:55:40.16488204Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
