            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-05T10:16:14.454770215Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Why this concern exists, and why the nine per-concern *-tests leaves are mis-scoped.\n\nlibedit ships NO test suite. Measured: Makefile.am line 9 is `SUBDIRS = src $(EXAMPLES_DIR) doc`, and there is no check_PROGRAMS, no TESTS, no test directory anywhere in the tarball. Every *-tests leaf in this plan is titled \"Port and extend tests for X\", and the port-wave-3-tests playbook describes porting a test set and using the literal port as an oracle for translation bugs. Neither is available. Those tests must be WRITTEN from the sem rules, not translated, which is a substantially larger job than the titles imply. Retitle them.\n\nThat matters because of what the evidence currently rests on. 711 rules are specified and 711 are covered, but `tested` is 0 for every one of them, and the workspace has 29 tests against roughly 35,000 lines of translated Rust. Every claim made so far -- 357 errata dispositions \"carried out\", defects \"reproduced faithfully\", rules \"verified against the C\" -- comes from reading code, not running it. The cross-checks caught real errors, so it is not worthless, but it is not the same class of evidence as an execution.\n\nTHE ORACLE IS THE IN-TREE C, NOT THE SYSTEM LIBRARY. This is the load-bearing decision.\n\nDebian ships libedit 3.1-20250104 as libedit.so.2.0.75. Our source is libedit-20260512-3.1, LT_VERSION 0:78:0 -- roughly sixteen months and three libtool revisions newer. Diffing against the system library would attribute upstream changes to the port. The tree has a working configure/Makefile.am, confirmed to build, so building src/*.c ourselves gives a reference at exactly the version we translated, from the same code, with the spec annotations being comments that change nothing. Zero version skew.\n\nThe system libraries stay useful for a narrower job: /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libedit.so.2 says what a deployed consumer actually links against, libreadline.so.8 is the reference for the GNU divergences the register logs, and libbsd.so.0 answers the open question of whether our NetBSD-derived vis matches the libbsd vis that wrote every history file already on a Debian user's disk.\n\nTiering, ordered by what is reachable today:\n\n- conformance-oracle and conformance-abi-shape and conformance-differential all run NOW. None of them need a terminal.\n- conformance-pty is blocked on platform-build and says so in its deps: without tcgetattr there is no raw mode, without TIOCGWINSZ there is no size, so the interactive path cannot be driven at all yet.\n\nDeterminism is the hard part of the differential tiers and must be designed in, not retrofitted: pin TERM and the TERMINFO database, pin LC_ALL, pin the window size, control HOME so no real ~/.editrc is read, and control every path the history file touches. libedit reads the environment in more places than is obvious -- see the secure_getenv divergence group.\n"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-05T13:15:52.105673744Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "A blind spot worth knowing about, surfaced by abi-narrow's commit (8af6682).\n\nThe test-footprint gate measures unit tests as a ratio of production code, per leaf directory, by counting #[cfg(test)] scope. It cannot see conformance/ at all -- that harness is shell plus a C driver plus #[ignore]d integration tests, none of which register as test code against crates/nshedit/src.\n\nSo the two measurements now disagree about the same code, and both are right about what they measure:\n\n- crates/nshedit/src sits at 0.020 against a floor of 0.200, and abi-narrow made it marginally worse (0.021 to 0.020) by adding production code and no unit tests.\n- Those exact paths are the ones conformance-differential drives, and it went from 2 divergences to 0 across 247 operations per locale against an oracle built from this tree's own C. For reproducing C behaviour that is stronger evidence than a unit test, because the oracle is the specification.\n\nDo not resolve this by lowering the floor, and do not resolve it by treating the differential as a substitute for unit tests. They answer different questions. The differential proves we match the C on the paths it drives; it says nothing about paths it does not reach, and it cannot localise a failure the way a unit test does. A 0.020 core is a real gap.\n\nWhat would actually help, in rough order of value:\n- Make the harness's coverage legible. If the gate could be told which rules the differential exercises, the number would stop being misleading in both directions -- right now it understates coverage on driven paths and hides the absence of coverage everywhere else.\n- The nspec `tested` facet is 0 for all 711 rules. That is the measurement that would show which rules have evidence of any kind, and nothing populates it yet. It is the better instrument for this project than a line-count ratio.\n\nRecorded so the bypass on 8af6682 is not read as a shrug. The gate is right that the core is under-tested; it is wrong that this commit made things less verified."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-06T13:12:52.677948619Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "How the harness claims a rule as tested, established by probing rather than by reading docs. Two measurements exist and THEY DISAGREE, which is the thing to know before annotating anything at scale.\n\nWHAT WORKS FOR nplan_spec_status. Adding `test_include` globs to the target-impl block, with @c and @bash in `languages`, makes nspec scan the C driver and the shell stages verify-only: a `/test` annotation there counts, a bare impl annotation is ignored. Measured: a `// [spec:libedit:sem:histedit.history-init-fn/test]` in conformance/driver/hist_tok.c and a `# [spec:libedit:sem:vis.strvisx-fn/test]` in conformance/vis-cross.sh took `tested` from 0 to 3. Both comment syntaxes parse.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT WORK FOR THE PORT GATE. nplan_port_check's wave-3 gate ignores test_include entirely — it reads only the target impl's `include` globs. The same three annotations left wave 3 at 78/650, unchanged. Moving one `/test` annotation into crates/nshedit-abi/tests/conformance.rs, which `crates/**/*.rs` already matches, took it 78 to 79 and dropped incomplete_total 572 to 571. That is the discriminating experiment; it is not inference.\n\nSo the gate-visible annotation site is a Rust file under crates/, and the natural one already exists: crates/nshedit-abi/tests/conformance.rs is structured one test function per conformance stage. A stage verifies many rules, so one test function carries many `/test` annotations, and the claim sits on the function `cargo test` actually runs. Annotating there satisfies both measurements at once.\n\nTHE WEAKNESS, which must not be glossed: those tests are #[ignore]d because they need a C toolchain. A rule marked tested by one of them is verified by a test that does NOT run in a default `cargo test --workspace`. That is defensible — ./conformance/run.sh is the real entry point and CI has a toolchain — but a `tested` count built on ignored tests is weaker than the number suggests, and anyone reading the figure deserves to know.\n\nThe sidecar was tried first and abandoned. .config/nplan/config.styx accepts `annotations { mode @both }`, whose docs name plan/annotations.styx as the sidecar source. The file's schema is documented nowhere I could find — not in nplan_schema, which is the plan schema, and not in the nplan skill. A plausible `claims (...)` shape was accepted without error and had no effect on coverage, which is the worst failure mode available: silent acceptance. Revisit only with the actual schema in hand.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR WAVE 3. 572 functions need a `/test` facet and 0 have one. The drivers can carry real claims for what they provably drive, but ONLY for the rules they drive — hist_tok.c reaches history and tokenizer, not chared, and annotating a function no driver executes would make the gate report coverage that does not exist. That is worse than a low number. Build conformance-driver-el, -readline and -ub first, then annotate exactly what each provably reaches, and hand-write tests for the remainder."
            }
        )
    }

