            {
                id nshterm-tests
                title "Test the crate we now own, which arrived with a test-to-production ratio of 0.015"
                deps (nshterm-extract)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T12:16:48.605511787Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Debt inherited at extraction, measured by the test-footprint gate: crates/nshterm/src/parser has a test-to-production ratio of 0.015 against a floor of 0.200. Bypassed at the extraction commit because it was true of term 1.2.1 before we touched it and fixing it was not that node's job. It is our problem now.\n\nWhat actually came across: 9 tests and 28 compiled terminfo fixtures under tests/data (linux and its variants, rxvt and its variants, screen, vt100, xterm, xterm-256color, dumb). They pass. The compiled parser gets a real workout from those fixtures; parm::expand does not, and that is the gap that matters.\n\nparm::expand is the one to aim at. The complexity gate puts it at cyclomatic 130 against a threshold of 50 -- it is an interpreter for terminfo's parameterised-string language, with a stack, variables, arithmetic, conditionals and printf-style formatting. Almost none of that is covered. It is also where nshterm-padding lands, so tests come first or that change is unverifiable.\n\nWorth knowing when writing them: ncurses' own test suite and the terminfo(5) grammar are the reference, and the C's `tputs`/`tgoto` behaviour is already specified in our rules -- sem:terminal.tgoto-fn and sem:terminal.tputs-fn -- so those give concrete expectations rather than guesses. The conformance harness cannot help here: it exercises libedit's surface, and nothing in the current driver reaches a parameterised capability.\n\nDo not lower the floor to make this pass. The ratio is a fact about a crate we intend to publish."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T18:13:18.991487927Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T18:15:40.285537671Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-05T11:54:12.645154602Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Why this crate exists, recorded because the reasoning changed twice and the final answer is not the obvious one.\n\ndec:libedit:terminal-caps-via-term-crate chose term 1.2.1 to replace the libtinfo dependency, and that direction stands -- terminal capabilities come from terminfo, never from a linked C library. What changed is the vehicle.\n\nterm is abandoned. Stebalien/term#93, \"[LFM] Looking For Maintainer\", has been open since November 2018; the author took a job and said he had little time or motivation left for it. So the three changes we need have no path upstream, and \"fork it, PR it, un-fork later\" -- which I recommended -- was never available.\n\ntermcolor is the ecosystem's answer for term's users and is NOT ours. Measured against docs.rs: it provides StandardStream, Buffer/BufferWriter, Ansi/NoColor, ColorSpec, HyperlinkSpec, and explicitly does not read the terminfo database, move the cursor, clear to end of line, insert or delete lines, or query dimensions. We never used term for colour. We use exactly two things from it -- term::terminfo::TermInfo and term::terminfo::parm::{Param, Variables, expand} -- and libedit needs 39 string capabilities plus 8 numeric and boolean ones. termcolor supplies none of them and has no API that could. The advice is right for the majority of term's users and does not transfer to us.\n\nThat is what makes the split clean rather than arbitrary: the half termcolor legitimately supersedes is exactly the half we drop. TerminfoTerminal, the Terminal trait, Attr, color and win.rs go; TermInfo, parm, the compiled parser, the name tables and the searcher stay. Verified before extracting: TerminfoTerminal is the only thing in terminfo/* reaching for crate::{Attr, color, Terminal, win}.\n\nThe alternative was writing a terminfo reader from scratch -- the compiled format is documented in term(5) and is maybe 300 lines. Rejected because the fork gives a working parser today and the name tables are 553 lines of data nobody should retype. The third option, the terminfo crate at 0.9.0, is WTFPL and was already rejected on that ground.\n\nFour leaves follow. Extraction first, then the three changes upstream could not take -- the set-uid env guard, the discarded padding delays, the hashed database -- plus the termcap name column ncurses carries in Caps and term dropped. The last two are the ones that make this crate worth publishing rather than merely worth having.\n\nLicence: MIT OR Apache-2.0, copyright The Rust Project Developers, carried forward. The workspace root says BSD-3-Clause, which is wrong for this crate and must not be inherited."
            }
        )
    }
)
